import{_ as o,o as t,c as s,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.YKbyQ3zk.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Security model","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"security-model.md","filePath":"security-model.md"}'),a={name:"security-model.md"};function i(r,e,d,c,h,l){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="security-model" tabindex="-1">Security model <a class="header-anchor" href="#security-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security model&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page is for a developer or security reviewer deciding whether EVO_AUTH fits their app. It says how the sign-in works, what protects it, and what it does not protect against. The limits are listed in plain words: read them before you ship.</p><h2 id="the-cookies" tabindex="-1">The cookies <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cookies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The cookies&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Sign-in uses three long-lived cookies, all first-party to your app, one short-lived cookie for the sign-in in progress, with passkeys on one for the passkey ceremony in progress, and with an email setting on one for the emailed code or link in progress. Names are shown with the default prefix <code>evo</code>. With <code>cookies.devMode</code> on (http on localhost only) the <code>__Host-</code> and <code>__Secure-</code> prefixes and the <code>Secure</code> flag are dropped.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Cookie</th><th>Name</th><th>Holds</th><th>Script can read it</th><th>Path</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Session</td><td><code>__Host-evo_session</code></td><td>The access token: a short-lived ES256 JWT (<code>accessToken.lifetimeSeconds</code>, 300 seconds by default).</td><td>No (<code>HttpOnly</code>)</td><td><code>/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Refresh</td><td><code>__Secure-evo_refresh</code></td><td>The refresh token <code>{sid}.{gen}.{nonce}.{mac}</code>.</td><td>No (<code>HttpOnly</code>)</td><td><code>{basePath}</code> only</td></tr><tr><td>uat</td><td><code>__Host-evo_uat</code></td><td><code>{signedInSeconds}.{sessionId}</code>, or <code>0</code> when signed out. Says whether a session exists, so a page knows to try the handshake.</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Sign-in (sealed)</td><td><code>__Host-evo_oauth</code></td><td>The sign-in attempt in progress (its state, PKCE verifier and OpenID Connect nonce among it), sealed with a key derived from <code>EVO_AUTH_SECRET</code>. Lives 600 seconds and is cleared when the sign-in finishes.</td><td>No</td><td><code>/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Passkey (sealed)</td><td><code>__Host-evo_passkey</code></td><td>One passkey ceremony&#39;s challenge, its purpose (add, sign in or confirm) and, to add or confirm, the session id, sealed under its own name so it never opens as the sign-in cookie. Lives 120 seconds and is cleared by the ceremony&#39;s verify request, whatever its outcome.</td><td>No</td><td><code>/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Email (sealed)</td><td><code>__Host-evo_email</code></td><td>One emailed code or link&#39;s challenge: its id, a binding to this browser and the address&#39;s rate-limit subject, sealed under its own name. Lives 600 seconds and is cleared when the sign-in, reset or address change finishes.</td><td>No (<code>HttpOnly</code>)</td><td><code>/</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>An access token is verified with the public keys alone: the ES256 signature, then <code>iss</code>, <code>aud</code>, <code>v</code>, the type and limits of every other claim, and <code>nbf</code> and <code>exp</code>. A claim the verifier does not know is ignored and never returned to your code, and every claim it knows is checked as before, so a newer server can add a claim without older apps refusing its tokens.</p><p>The refresh cookie&#39;s path keeps it off page routes, so only the auth endpoints ever see it. The server never returns a refresh value to a page.</p><p>A page request with an expired access token and a <code>uat</code> that names a session gets a 307 to <code>{basePath}/handshake</code>. The handshake rotates the refresh token, sets the cookies and sends the browser back once. A browser that is prefetching is refused, so a prefetch never rotates a token. A loop guard ends a repeated handshake signed out.</p><h2 id="rotation-and-the-grace-window" tabindex="-1">Rotation and the grace window <a class="header-anchor" href="#rotation-and-the-grace-window" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rotation and the grace window&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each refresh issues a new refresh token and retires the old one. The immediately previous token may be exchanged again for <code>refresh.reuseGraceSeconds</code> (30 by default, at most 60). That window exists for concurrent reloads and for a response that never arrived.</p><p>A token older than that, used again, is treated as stolen: the session is revoked as <code>refresh_reuse</code>. <strong>At 0, the window is off</strong>, and every lost response, double reload or restored tab group ends the session and costs the user a sign-in.</p><h2 id="openid-connect-id_tokens" tabindex="-1">OpenID Connect id_tokens <a class="header-anchor" href="#openid-connect-id_tokens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OpenID Connect id_tokens&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A provider made with <code>oidc()</code> gets a signed id_token from the service&#39;s token endpoint. EVO_AUTH treats it as hostile until every check below has passed, in this order, and the first failure ends the sign-in with <code>oauth_failed</code>, no session, and only the sign-in cookie cleared. Nothing the token or the service said reaches a log line, an error or an event.</p><ol><li>It is a compact JWS: three base64url parts, the header and the payload JSON objects.</li><li>Its <code>alg</code> is <code>RS256</code> or <code>ES256</code>, read from the header and held to that list, never taken from a key. <code>none</code>, every <code>HS*</code> and anything else are refused before a key is looked at, so a token signed with HMAC under the service&#39;s public key goes nowhere. A <code>crit</code> header is refused.</li><li>The JWKS at <code>jwksUri</code> is fetched at every sign-in, with no redirect followed, a 10-second timeout and a 1 MiB cap, and nothing of it is kept.</li><li>The candidate keys are those of the algorithm&#39;s type, with that <code>alg</code> and <code>use</code> <code>sig</code> when a key names them, so a key marked <code>use: &quot;enc&quot;</code> is never used. They are narrowed by <code>kid</code> when the header has one; the <code>kid</code> only picks keys. An RSA key whose modulus is shorter than 2048 bits is not used either, as RFC 7518 section 3.3 requires for <code>RS256</code>; its size is read from the key&#39;s <code>n</code> before it is imported. The signature must verify against one of the keys left.</li><li><code>iss</code> equals the configured issuer exactly; for <code>google()</code>, either of the two forms Google documents, <code>https://accounts.google.com</code> and <code>accounts.google.com</code>. <code>aud</code> contains the client id, and when <code>aud</code> holds more than one value, or <code>azp</code> is present, <code>azp</code> equals the client id.</li><li><code>exp</code> is later than now minus <code>clockSkewSeconds</code>, <code>iat</code> no later than now plus it, and <code>sub</code> is 1 to 255 characters.</li><li><code>nonce</code> equals the nonce sealed in the sign-in cookie at the start, compared in constant time.</li><li>For a reverification, a present <code>auth_time</code> must be no earlier than the reverification&#39;s start minus <code>clockSkewSeconds</code>.</li></ol><p><code>at_hash</code> is not checked: the token comes straight from the token endpoint over TLS. With a <code>userinfoEndpoint</code>, the user-info answer&#39;s <code>sub</code> must equal the id_token&#39;s. Every answer a provider request reads, of any kind, is cut off past 1 MiB.</p><h2 id="the-client-address-and-the-three-modes" tabindex="-1">The client address and the three modes <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-client-address-and-the-three-modes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The client address and the three modes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rate limits count per client address. The auth side learns the address in one of three ways, chosen by whether <code>EVO_AUTH_PROXY_SECRET</code> is set, never by anything in the request.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>When</th><th>Where the address comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>proxy</code></td><td>The proxy secret is set.</td><td>Only <code>X-Evo-Client-IP</code>, and only on a request carrying the right <code>X-Evo-Proxy-Secret</code>. Anything else is <code>proxy_refused</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>connection_address</code></td><td>No proxy secret; the adapter passes <code>{ source: &quot;connection&quot;, address }</code>.</td><td>The connection&#39;s own address. No header is read.</td></tr><tr><td><code>convex_dev_metadata</code></td><td>No proxy secret and <code>cookies.devMode</code> on.</td><td>Convex&#39;s request metadata. On a local deployment it is the leftmost <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, which a client sets freely.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On Convex outside the development cookie mode, the proxy is mandatory. In proxy mode, a caller who skips the proxy and calls the Convex site directly costs one setup per request and is refused. See <a href="./proxy-and-client-address.html">The proxy and the client address</a>.</p><h2 id="revocation" tabindex="-1">Revocation <a class="header-anchor" href="#revocation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Revocation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Revoking a session, banning a user, signing out everywhere and deleting an account are flag-first: the flag is set in one write, so access ends at once, and the rows are cleaned up in batches. The auth routes check the session on every request. A Convex function checks only the token, so a token issued before a revocation still works there until it expires.</p><p>Removing a way to sign in ends the user&#39;s other sessions. When a user disconnects an account, removes a passkey or turns off two-step sign-in, every other session of theirs is revoked as <code>credential_removed</code> in the same operation that deletes the row, and the session they did it from stays signed in. Someone who got in through a compromised account is signed out when the user disconnects it, even if they later confirmed their identity some other way. When an admin or the command line removes a user&#39;s passkey or second factor, all of that user&#39;s sessions end. On Convex, an access token already issued keeps working until it expires (<code>accessToken.lifetimeSeconds</code>, 300 seconds by default), the same limit as signing out everywhere.</p><h2 id="the-admin-routes" tabindex="-1">The admin routes <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-admin-routes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The admin routes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The routes under <code>{basePath}/admin/</code> (nine; with passkeys on, one more that removes a user&#39;s passkey; with <code>secondFactor.enabled</code> on, one more that removes a user&#39;s second factor; with <code>usernames.enabled</code> on, one more that clears a user&#39;s username; with organisations on, nine more for organisations, their members and their invitations; twenty-one with all four on) are reachable from a browser, so they are checked on the server, in this order, on every request: the path (with <code>admins.userIds</code> empty the paths do not exist at all), the method, the Origin check for a POST, the Bearer access token, whether the token&#39;s user is in <code>admins.userIds</code>, whether the session is live in storage (not banned, not deleted, not revoked), the <code>admin_read</code> or <code>admin_write</code> bucket, the input, the target, and last, for ban, unban, delete, removing a second factor, clearing a username and deleting an organisation, the recent confirmation of the admin&#39;s identity.</p><ul><li><strong>A failed admin check looks like a missing path.</strong> A missing or bad token, a user who is not listed, and a listed user whose session has ended all get the same <code>404 not_found</code> as a path that does not exist, with the same body and headers. The method and the Origin are checked first, so while <code>admins.userIds</code> lists anyone a wrong method is <code>405</code> and a POST from another origin is <code>403</code>, which shows only that the deployment lists admins. The token and list checks make no storage call; the session check makes one read whatever the reason. Admin status is never read from a token and never comes from app code: the list is in the config, and changing it needs a deploy.</li><li><strong>An admin cannot act on themselves or on another admin.</strong> Such a write is <code>400 invalid_request</code>, decided before any confirmation with the provider, so an admin is never sent to the provider for an action that will be refused.</li><li><strong>One exception to &quot;a refusal never says whether a user exists&quot;.</strong> Once a caller has passed the admin check, a route may answer <code>404 not_found</code> for a target user or session that does not exist. This applies to listed admins only.</li><li><strong>What is sent, and what is never sent.</strong> An admin response carries a user&#39;s email and each linked account&#39;s email and <code>emailVerified</code>, for the admin to read; no decision about who a user is or what they may do reads an email, and every action names its user by the stored id. No admin response carries <code>privateMetadata</code>, an address HMAC, a nonce hash, a generation or other MAC material, <code>purgeAt</code> or <code>eventsUntil</code>, a provider token, a secret, the lowercase search forms <code>loginLower</code> and <code>emailLower</code>, or the admins list beyond the <code>isAdmin</code> flag on each user of the list and on user detail. Every response is built from a list of allowed fields and carries <code>Cache-Control: private, no-store</code>.</li><li><strong>An admin can clear a username and nothing more.</strong> The route takes only <code>{ userId }</code> and removes the name; no admin route sets or changes one, so an admin cannot give a person a name or take one for themselves through another account. The name is free at once.</li><li><strong>The record of who acted is kept.</strong> Every admin write records <code>actorUserId</code>, the admin. Deleting a user removes that user&#39;s own events but keeps every event an admin wrote about them until the event retention expires, so deleting a user does not erase the record of who deleted them. Such a kept event may name a session whose row was deleted with its user; nothing reads a session through an event.</li></ul><h2 id="linking-accounts" tabindex="-1">Linking accounts <a class="header-anchor" href="#linking-accounts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Linking accounts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Linking attaches the account the browser is signed in to at the provider when the user clicks Connect. The link starts with a same-origin POST, so another site cannot begin one. A provider that cannot force a fresh sign-in (any <code>oauth2()</code> provider) may attach whichever account is signed in at that provider in this browser. After linking, the profile page shows which account was connected, and the user can disconnect it. Accounts are never linked by email.</p><ul><li><strong>Why never by email.</strong> A provider is app-defined, so a misconfigured or hostile provider could assert someone else&#39;s verified email. Joining users on a matching email would let it take over that person&#39;s account. Joining only on the user&#39;s own request, from a signed-in session, needs no trust in any provider&#39;s email; no decision about identity or access reads an email.</li><li><strong>What the link start checks.</strong> <code>POST {basePath}/{provider}/link</code> needs the Origin check, the Bearer access token, a live session and a confirmation with the provider within <code>reverification.maxAgeSeconds</code>, and counts toward <code>self_service</code>. The <code>GET</code> start refuses <code>intent=link</code> with <code>400 invalid_request</code> and sets no cookie.</li><li><strong>What the callback checks.</strong> The usual state, PKCE and, for <code>oidc()</code>, id_token and nonce checks; then the session sealed in the attempt must still be live, its user neither banned nor deleted, and its confirmation still recent. An account another user holds, and a second account at a provider the user already has one at, are refused with <code>oauth_failed</code> and change nothing. A completed link writes only the account row and <code>account_linked</code>, never creates, switches or rotates a session, and clears the confirmation, so each link needs its own.</li><li><strong>Unlinking</strong> is a self-service POST behind the same confirmation. The last account cannot be removed. Unlinking ends every other session of the user (see <a href="#revocation">Revocation</a>). Linking and unlinking each record an event with the user, the provider and the provider&#39;s account id, and no session id.</li></ul><h2 id="passkeys" tabindex="-1">Passkeys <a class="header-anchor" href="#passkeys" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Passkeys&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With <code>passkeys.enabled</code> on, a signed-in user can add a passkey and then sign in or confirm their identity with it. The routes under <code>{basePath}/passkeys</code> follow the self-service rules: the Origin check on every POST, and for every route but sign-in the Bearer access token and a live session. With passkeys off the paths do not exist.</p><ul><li><strong>The challenge lives only in the browser&#39;s cookie.</strong> Each ceremony&#39;s 32 random bytes go out in the options and are kept only in the sealed passkey cookie, bound to the ceremony and, to add or confirm, to the session. An options request writes nothing to storage. A verify request refuses a missing, expired or foreign cookie, and clears it whatever the outcome, so a challenge is used once. A replay needs the victim&#39;s <code>HttpOnly</code> cookie and the signed response together, within 120 seconds; once the passkey&#39;s signature counter is above 0, a replayed response is refused by the counter rule as well. This limit is accepted rather than closed with a stored challenge because winning that race needs the victim&#39;s <code>HttpOnly</code> passkey cookie and their signed response at once, and anyone holding both can already sign in with them and take the session cookie that comes back, so it gives an attacker nothing they did not already have.</li><li><strong>Verification comes before storage.</strong> The browser&#39;s response is checked against the origin of <code>publicUrl</code> exactly and against <code>passkeys.rpId</code>, with user verification required, before any storage write. Registration asks for attestation <code>none</code>, so it trusts the authenticator for no claim about its make; the credential id inside the signed authenticator data must equal the one the browser reports, and an id longer than 1,023 bytes is refused.</li><li><strong>A passkey sign-in finds the user by the credential alone.</strong> The credential id&#39;s SHA-256 is looked up; the user handle must be present and must be the owner&#39;s handle, and never picks the user by itself. The owner&#39;s handle is the one a passkey sign-up gave them, or for a user who never signed up with a passkey, their id, so passkeys added before a handle existed keep working. Confirming identity and the second step compare it the same way and, as before, accept a response with no handle. A banned or deleted user is refused. Every failed passkey check answers the same <code>400 invalid_request</code>, whatever the cause, and the log names the cause by a code only, never a credential id, key, counter, challenge or user id.</li><li><strong>The counter rule.</strong> Once a passkey&#39;s stored counter is above 0, a returned counter that is not greater, 0 included, is refused as a sign the authenticator may have been cloned. A synced passkey reports 0 every time and passes.</li><li><strong>Adding a passkey uses up a recent confirmation</strong>, exactly as linking an account does, and removing one needs a recent confirmation without using it up. A user holds at most 20 passkeys. The last way to sign in, accounts and passkeys counted together, cannot be removed by the user or by an admin; an admin removal is recorded with the admin as actor. A user&#39;s removal ends their other sessions, and an admin&#39;s ends all of the user&#39;s sessions (see <a href="#revocation">Revocation</a>).</li><li><strong>Every passkey sign-in completion counts toward <code>passkey_signin</code></strong>, per client address, before the passkey is looked at.</li><li><strong><code>passkeys.rpId</code></strong> may name a parent domain of <code>publicUrl</code>&#39;s host. There is no public-suffix check: a wrong value fails safe, because the browser refuses the ceremony.</li><li><strong>The sign-in page follows only a relative way back.</strong> On a shared sign-in server the chooser links to the hub&#39;s sign-in page with <code>redirect</code> set to the way back to its authorize request. <code>SignIn</code> follows that value only when it is a single relative path the redirect rule accepts, never an absolute address, so the link cannot send a person off the hub; anything else is ignored. With <code>intent=reverify</code> it confirms the signed-in person with their own passkey and never starts a sign-in, so the link cannot swap the hub session for another person&#39;s.</li></ul><h2 id="passkey-sign-up" tabindex="-1">Passkey sign-up <a class="header-anchor" href="#passkey-sign-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Passkey sign-up&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With <code>passkeys.signUp</code> on, a new person signs up with an address and a passkey (see <a href="./passkeys.html#signing-up-with-a-passkey">Signing up with a passkey</a>).</p><ul><li><strong>The account exists only after the passkey is verified.</strong> <code>/start</code> and <code>/options</code> write only the sign-up&#39;s challenge row; <code>/verify</code> makes the user, the verified email account and the passkey, deletes the row and starts the session, in one storage operation. Two verifies of one sign-up make one user; the other is answered <code>address_taken</code>. An address that has an email account by then is answered <code>address_taken</code>, and a credential already stored <code>passkey_exists</code>, both without writing anything.</li><li><strong>An address still does not say whether it has an account.</strong> <code>/start</code> answers and works the same for every address. The email job decides: an address with an account gets the existing-account notice, its row keeps no code or link hash, and every code then counts as wrong until the fifth deletes the row, exactly as for an unknown address. A banned or deleted owner is sent nothing.</li><li><strong>The proof is bound to the browser that asked.</strong> The code and link work only with the email cookie, which holds the row&#39;s id and a binding to this browser. A code from a browser without it is the generic 404 and counts nothing; a link opened elsewhere answers <code>email_other_browser</code> and counts nothing, telling its holder only what the link already does. The first code or link marks the row proved, removes both hashes, resets its tries and gives it 5 minutes; the email cookie is sealed again to that time.</li><li><strong>The passkey&#39;s challenge is bound to the sign-up.</strong> The passkey cookie carries the purpose <code>sign_up</code> and the row&#39;s id, so <code>/verify</code> needs both cookies to name the same row. A missing, expired or foreign passkey cookie is refused without counting; a registration that fails its checks counts toward the row&#39;s 5 tries.</li><li><strong>The user handle is random.</strong> It is 16 random bytes as 22 base64url characters, made by the route and stored on the user. It never changes; deleting the user clears it with the rest of their data, and a later sign-up with the same address makes a new one.</li><li><strong>No second step at sign-up.</strong> The person has just proved the address and made a passkey that requires user verification, so the session starts directly, even with <code>secondFactor.required</code> on, and <code>sign_in_account</code> is not counted, as for a passkey sign-in.</li><li><strong>Accepted:</strong> someone at an unattended browser on the &quot;Create your passkey&quot; step can finish the sign-up with their own passkey, as with any sign-up left open; that step lives 5 minutes.</li></ul><h2 id="email-codes-links-and-passwords" tabindex="-1">Email codes, links and passwords <a class="header-anchor" href="#email-codes-links-and-passwords" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Email codes, links and passwords&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These apply while <code>emailAuth.code</code>, <code>emailAuth.link</code> or <code>emailAuth.password</code> is on (see <a href="./email.html">Email</a> and <a href="./email-password.html">Email and password</a>).</p><ul><li><strong>Signing in by an emailed code or link is not NIST-grade.</strong> NIST SP 800-63B-4 does not allow email for out-of-band authentication. It is offered because most sign-in products offer it and because anyone who controls a mailbox can already reset a password there. Whoever can read the mailbox, or your mail service, can sign in as that person. Leave <code>emailAuth.code</code> and <code>emailAuth.link</code> off if you must meet NIST.</li><li><strong>Only hashes are stored.</strong> A code is hashed with a key derived from the master secret and the challenge&#39;s id, and a link token with the same key, so a copy of the database holds neither. No table, log line, event or job argument holds a code, a link token or a link, and no log line holds an address: the job that sends a message is given only the challenge&#39;s id. The one place a code and link exist is the message, so your sender must not log them.</li><li><strong>A code or link works once, for 10 minutes, in the browser that asked.</strong> A sealed <code>__Host-evo_email</code> cookie (with the default prefix) holds the challenge&#39;s id and a binding to the browser; it lives 10 minutes and is <code>HttpOnly</code>. Five wrong codes void the challenge, and a new code voids the earlier ones for that address and purpose. A link needs a button press, so a mail scanner cannot use it up, and opened in another browser it signs nobody in. <code>POST {basePath}/email/verify</code> takes sign-in purposes only and ignores any bearer token, so a signed-in browser cannot turn a sign-in code into a confirmation.</li><li><strong>An address does not say whether it has an account.</strong> The code, sign-up, reset and add-address routes answer the same for every address and do the same work, and the decision whether to send is made by a job after the answer has gone, so timing says nothing either. A refused password sign-in does the same work for every address too: an address with no password is checked against a fixed dummy hash, and one storage operation records the failure. The only messages that name an account are the two notices, sent to the address itself.</li><li><strong>Passwords are slow and peppered.</strong> PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 at 600,000 iterations, a random salt, then an HMAC keyed by <code>EVO_AUTH_PASSWORD_PEPPER</code>. Hashing runs in the handler, never in storage. A stored hash that names more than 2,000,000 iterations is refused before anything is derived. Replacing the pepper makes every user reset their password.</li><li><strong>Guessing is throttled, then stopped.</strong> Failed passwords count per address (10 in 15 minutes) and per client address (30 in 15 minutes), and sign-ups, by password and by passkey, count per client address (10 an hour). The 100th failed password in a row turns the password off and emails the person. Only a reset by email, or a change by a signed-in person who has just confirmed their identity, turns it back on; no admin can. The refusal is the same generic <code>invalid_credentials</code> whether the password is wrong or off.</li><li><strong>A reset checks the code before it does any work.</strong> The password rules and the hash run only for a right code, and a refusal for the rules uses one of the code&#39;s five tries. A reset or a change ends the user&#39;s other sessions with the reason <code>credential_changed</code>.</li><li><strong>One confirmation covers one change.</strong> Setting or changing a password, and adding or changing an address, use up a recent confirmation when they start. Removals keep using the recent window.</li><li><strong>The breach check can be skipped.</strong> A new password is checked against Pwned Passwords by the first 5 hexadecimal characters of its SHA-1 only. When the service cannot be reached the check is skipped and the password is accepted, with <code>password.breach_check_unavailable</code> in the log. Set <code>password.breachCheck</code> to <code>false</code> to turn the check off.</li><li><strong>An email account is never joined to another account because the address matches.</strong> A user has one email address, and changing it moves the password and ends the other sessions.</li></ul><h2 id="usernames" tabindex="-1">Usernames <a class="header-anchor" href="#usernames" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usernames&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These apply while <code>usernames.enabled</code> is on (see <a href="./email-password.html#usernames">Email and password</a>).</p><ul><li><strong>A username names the email account&#39;s password, and nothing more.</strong> Signing in with one checks the same password, signs in to the same session and asks for the same second step as the address. A user holds a name only while they have an email account; one without can still remove a name they hold.</li><li><strong>A refused username sign-in looks the same whatever the cause.</strong> An unknown name, a wrong password, a user with no password and a turned-off password all answer <code>invalid_credentials</code> after the same storage calls and the same hash, against a fixed dummy hash when there is none. A name that fails the public rule is answered at once with the same reason and no hash, so nobody can make the server hash a password for a name that cannot exist.</li><li><strong>The account bucket follows what was typed.</strong> <code>password_sign_in_account</code> counts a username&#39;s failures under the username&#39;s own hash and an address&#39;s under the address&#39;s, so ten wrong passwords for a name do not hold up sign-in by the address, nor tie the name to the address by the answer they cause. The per-client-address bucket is shared. The 100 failures in a row that turn a password off count both ways in together.</li><li><strong>Usernames are public.</strong> Anyone willing to make an account can learn which names are taken, and anyone who knows a name can turn that person&#39;s password off with 100 wrong tries, at most 10 every 15 minutes, until they reset it by email. Nothing may rely on a name being hard to find out.</li><li><strong>Changing or removing a name needs a recent confirmation.</strong> Without one, someone at an unlocked, signed-in browser with an account of their own could free the person&#39;s name and take it. The first name needs none, and a change or removal uses the confirmation up, so the next one asks again. One storage operation checks that a name is free and writes it, and every attempt with a well-formed body counts in the <code>username_change</code> bucket, 10 a day for each user.</li><li><strong>A deleted user&#39;s name is freed at once,</strong> in the same write that starts the deletion, so it is never held by an account being removed.</li><li><strong>Sign-up says nothing about a name before the mailbox is proved.</strong> <code>POST {basePath}/email/sign-up</code> with a taken name and with a free one gives the same answer, the same storage calls, the same work and the same email; the route never looks the name up. Only the completion, which needs the emailed code or link, sets the name or answers <code>usernameTaken</code>, and it does both in the one storage operation that makes the user, so two completions with one name leave one holder. A name that fails the rule or is reserved is refused before anything is counted, since those rules are public.</li></ul><h2 id="two-step-sign-in" tabindex="-1">Two-step sign-in <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-step-sign-in" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two-step sign-in&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With <code>secondFactor.enabled</code> on, a correct first factor can lead to a second step instead of a session. The routes are listed in <a href="./second-factor.html#the-routes">Two-step sign-in</a> and exist only while the setting is on.</p><ul><li><strong>A pending sign-in is not a half session.</strong> After the first factor there is a row, and no token and no session. The row holds the user, which first factor passed, the SHA-256 of a binding, an attempt count and an expiry of 10 minutes. A sealed <code>__Host-evo_signin</code> cookie (with the default prefix) carries the row&#39;s id and the binding itself. Storage keeps only the binding&#39;s hash, so a copy of the table cannot be turned into a cookie, and a stolen id alone can neither use a row nor use up its owner&#39;s attempts: a binding that does not match is refused as the generic 404 before anything is counted. The row can do nothing but complete, cancel or, under <code>required</code>, set up a factor.</li><li><strong>A completed step makes the session in the operation that deletes the row,</strong> so two correct requests make one session. A wrong answer counts on the row and in the two buckets in the same operation, and the fifth ends the row.</li><li><strong>A code works once.</strong> The authenticator&#39;s last accepted step is stored, a code for that step or an earlier one is refused, and the check and the update are one storage operation, so two requests carrying one code cannot both pass. A backup code&#39;s sealed value is removed in the operation that accepts it.</li><li><strong>Only sealed values are stored.</strong> An authenticator secret is 20 random bytes, sealed with AES-GCM under a key derived by HKDF from <code>EVO_AUTH_SECOND_FACTOR_KEY</code>, and a backup code is stored as a seal of the code&#39;s SHA-256 under the same key, not as a keyed hash. The route handler holds the key, and while the key is being replaced the previous one too; storage is given only sealed values, and compares sealed text and steps without opening anything, so no storage adapter, scheduled function or Convex component function sees a secret or a code. The app-exported reseal action holds the key, as the HTTP actions do. A copy of the database alone yields neither an authenticator secret nor a backup code that works. The key and the database together do yield the secrets, and let the backup codes be searched offline. A backup code has 50 bits, so it is only as good as the limits below.</li><li><strong>Guessing is throttled and never ends in a lock-out.</strong> A pending sign-in allows 5 wrong attempts. Wrong codes also count per user (10 in 15 minutes) and per client address (30 in 15 minutes), at the second step and when confirming identity alike. While a bucket is full a right code is refused too. There is no turn-off after repeated failures, because someone who has the password could use failures to push the real user into recovery. A code with 3 valid values in a million, at 10 tries every 15 minutes for one user, takes about a year to guess on average. These failures do not count toward a password&#39;s 100.</li><li><strong>Email is not a way back.</strong> If a mailbox could remove the second factor, the factor would add nothing against a mailbox takeover. Recovery is an admin, or <code>evo-auth-server users second-factor remove</code>, which ends all of the user&#39;s sessions and records the actor.</li><li><strong>The passkey sign-in options never turn a pending sign-in into an ordinary one.</strong> <code>POST {basePath}/passkeys/signin/options</code> takes no body, <code>{}</code> or <code>{ &quot;conditional&quot;: true }</code>, and refuses any other body with the generic <code>400</code> before it reads anything. With <code>{ &quot;conditional&quot;: true }</code>, the request the sign-in card&#39;s passkey autofill makes, it always answers the ordinary sign-in options and leaves the pending cookie and its row untouched, so reloading the sign-in page during a second step, or opening it in another tab, neither hands out the step&#39;s options nor cancels the step. Without it, the answer is the second step&#39;s options (that user&#39;s passkeys only, sealed to the pending row) only while the pending cookie names a live row; a cookie whose row is gone, expired or bound to another browser is cleared and the ordinary options are answered. Either way the step completes only at <code>/signin/second-factor/verify</code>, and an ordinary passkey sign-in starts a session of its own with no second step, as before.</li><li><strong>A credential or access change cancels the user&#39;s pending sign-ins.</strong> A password reset or change, removing or replacing the second factor, a ban, ending every session, deleting the user, unlinking an account, removing a passkey and changing the address each delete the user&#39;s pending sign-ins in the same operation, because a pending sign-in belongs to no session and could be an intruder&#39;s. So after a reset, someone who typed the old password just before it has no live pending sign-in, and, under <code>required</code>, for a user with no factor yet, cannot finish one with an authenticator of their own.</li><li><strong>Set-up during sign-in is bounded.</strong> Under a pending cookie, <code>start</code> and <code>confirm</code> count the <code>self_service</code> limit for the user. A wrong code at <code>confirm</code> counts nothing more, because the holder made that secret.</li><li><strong>While <code>secondFactor.enabled</code> is on, a user with a confirmed authenticator confirms identity only with a passkey, an authenticator code or a backup code.</strong> A password, a provider and an emailed code are refused for such a user, because each is weaker than signing in. With the setting off a password alone signs them in, so a leftover factor restricts nothing.</li><li><strong>The <code>vat</code> claim is the last confirmation of identity,</strong> in whole seconds, and is left out of a token whose session was never confirmed. It adds at most 17 bytes: with the worst case of every custom claim, <code>o</code> included, the claims come to 1,142 bytes of their 1,200-byte budget. An older verifier ignores it. Evo Auth&#39;s own routes never decide from it: they read the session row. In your own code, <code>requireRecentVerification</code> reads it, as old as the token, and a confirmation answers with a new token at once (see <a href="./react.html#recent-confirmation-in-your-own-actions">React</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="organisations" tabindex="-1">Organisations <a class="header-anchor" href="#organisations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Organisations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With <code>organizations.enabled</code> on, the routes under <code>{basePath}/organizations</code> follow the self-service rules: the Origin check on every POST, the Bearer access token and a live session. With it off the paths do not exist.</p><ul><li><strong>Permissions come from storage, never from the token.</strong> Each route calls one storage operation with the caller as the member, and the operation reads the membership and maps its role through <code>organizations.roles</code> in the same transaction. A member removed a moment ago is refused at once, even with a token that still has time left. The roles reach storage from the server&#39;s validated config, built by the same function the server API uses.</li><li><strong>A non-member learns nothing.</strong> Someone who is not a live member gets the same <code>404 not_found</code> as for an organisation that does not exist, on every route about it. A member whose role lacks the permission gets <code>forbidden</code>.</li><li><strong>Nobody grants beyond their own role.</strong> A member may give a role, or change or remove a member, only when that role holds no permission the member&#39;s own role lacks (<code>role_above_own</code>). App admins on the admin routes, and the server API, are exempt.</li><li><strong>Every organisation keeps a manager.</strong> A role change, removal or leaving that would leave no member holding <code>org:memberships:manage</code> is refused as <code>last_manager</code>, for members and admins alike.</li><li><strong>Deleting needs a recent confirmation</strong>, on the member&#39;s route and the admin&#39;s. Changing roles and removing members do not: both can be undone, and the last-manager rule prevents the damaging case.</li><li><strong>What is sent.</strong> Members see an organisation&#39;s name, slug, image and creation time, and members&#39; names, pictures and roles. Admins also see <code>publicMetadata</code> and the creator&#39;s id. <code>privateMetadata</code> is never sent, and no refusal or log line carries a value.</li><li><strong>The admin routes</strong> follow the order of checks above, with the admin recorded as <code>actorUserId</code> on every event.</li><li><strong>Invitation links are secrets that work once.</strong> A link carries 32 random bytes in base64url; storage keeps only their SHA-256, as for the hub&#39;s authorization codes. The link is answered once to its creator and given to <code>onInvitationCreated</code>, which runs after the invitation is saved, outside storage: only the token&#39;s hash is ever an argument of a storage operation or a Convex component function, so the link is in no row, scheduled function, auth event, log line or error. Preview and accept take the token in the JSON body, never in the address, so it stays out of server logs and the Referer header. Accepting adds the membership and deletes the invitation in one operation, so two accepts of one link add one member. A missing, expired, revoked or used link gets one <code>404 not_found</code>. A link expires after at most 30 days, 7 by default, and a sweep removes expired rows.</li><li><strong>An invitation grants no more than its creator could.</strong> A member needs <code>org:invitations:manage</code> and may invite only for a role that holds no permission their own role lacks, so a second account cannot get round the rule above. App admins and the server API are exempt. Invitations are never matched by email.</li><li><strong>A link stops working when its creator loses the right to grant it.</strong> Each invitation records whether a member, an app admin or the server API made it, and every preview and accept checks that creator again: a member must still be a member of the organisation, neither banned nor deleted, with a role that still holds <code>org:invitations:manage</code> and every permission of the invited role; an app admin must still be in <code>admins.userIds</code>, neither banned nor deleted. So a member who is removed, demoted, banned or deleted, or no longer allowed to invite, cannot rejoin through a link they made earlier, and nobody has to remember to revoke it. A link that fails this check gets the same <code>404 not_found</code> as a revoked one, so whoever holds it learns nothing about its creator. Links made through the server API are the app&#39;s own and are not checked this way.</li><li><strong><code>evo-auth check</code> prints the roles fingerprint</strong>, computed from the config alone as <code>evo-auth-server check</code> computes it. An app that checks permissions with its own copy of the config can compare the two.</li></ul><h2 id="organisations-the-active-organisation-and-the-o-claim" tabindex="-1">Organisations: the active organisation and the <code>o</code> claim <a class="header-anchor" href="#organisations-the-active-organisation-and-the-o-claim" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Organisations: the active organisation and the `o` claim&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With <code>organizations.enabled</code> on, each session can have one active organisation.</p><ul><li><strong>The claim names, it does not grant.</strong> The access token carries <code>o: { id, slg, rol }</code>: the organisation&#39;s id, its slug and the member&#39;s role key, never a permission list. Permissions are worked out from the role through the <code>organizations.roles</code> of the config doing the reading. The verifier refuses a token whose <code>o</code> is anything but those three strings within their limits. With organisations off, no token carries <code>o</code>.</li><li><strong>Only a live member can set it.</strong> <code>POST {basePath}/organizations/active</code> checks the membership in storage. Someone who is not a member gets the generic <code>not_found</code>, and nothing is written. The new token comes from a refresh-token rotation, so it needs the refresh cookie of the same session as the Bearer token.</li><li><strong>Every refresh reads storage again.</strong> A removed member&#39;s next token has no <code>o</code>, and the session forgets the organisation. A changed role or slug is in the next token. So a removal or a demotion reaches tokens within one access-token lifetime (<code>accessToken.lifetimeSeconds</code>).</li><li><strong>Evo Auth&#39;s own routes never decide from the claim.</strong> Every organisation route reads the membership from storage. In your own code, <code>hasPermission</code> reads the claim, and <code>requireMembership</code> on Convex reads storage, for a change that must not wait out a token&#39;s lifetime.</li><li><strong>A new session starts where the last one left off,</strong> at the user&#39;s last active organisation, but only while the user is still a member of it.</li></ul><h2 id="pending-actions-and-the-way-back-from-the-provider" tabindex="-1">Pending actions and the way back from the provider <a class="header-anchor" href="#pending-actions-and-the-way-back-from-the-provider" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pending actions and the way back from the provider&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every action that needs a recent confirmation with the provider and is started from the React components (deleting the account, connecting or disconnecting an account in <code>UserProfile</code>, and banning, unbanning or deleting a user in <code>AdminPanel</code>) is carried across the round trip the same way. Before the browser leaves, the page stores the signed-in user&#39;s id, the action (and for the admin page the target user&#39;s id), a random 16-byte nonce and an expiry ten minutes on in this tab&#39;s <code>sessionStorage</code>, and the address back carries only the nonce. On return, the confirmation opens only when the nonce matches the stored entry exactly, the entry has not expired and it is the signed-in user&#39;s; the entry is deleted and the marker removed from the address either way, and the action itself still waits for a click.</p><ul><li><strong>A link cannot open a confirmation.</strong> Someone who sends a signed-in user a link with a marker cannot know the nonce, so the page renders as a plain visit, with no confirmation and no &quot;You confirmed&quot; line. <code>sessionStorage</code> belongs to one tab, so a link opened in another tab matches nothing either.</li><li><strong>The address says nothing about the action.</strong> The address sent to the provider names neither the action nor its target. The <code>evo_profile</code>, <code>evo_connect</code> and <code>evo_linked</code> markers only choose what to show and are removed once read; the &quot;Connected&quot; line after a link names the account <code>GET /me</code> lists as the most recently linked, never one the address names.</li><li><strong>When storage is refused</strong>, the round trip still happens and nothing opens on return; the next click goes through because the confirmation is fresh.</li><li><strong>Not a defence against injected script</strong>, which can read the entry or send the request itself (limit 6 below).</li></ul><h2 id="a-content-security-policy" tabindex="-1">A content security policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-content-security-policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A content security policy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A strict policy limits what injected script can do. The recipe the example app ships:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes evo-light evo-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>default-src &#39;self&#39;; script-src &#39;self&#39; &#39;nonce-&lt;per-response nonce&gt;&#39;; style-src &#39;self&#39;; img-src &#39;self&#39; https: data:;</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>connect-src &#39;self&#39; &lt;your Convex origin&gt; &lt;its WebSocket origin&gt;; object-src &#39;none&#39;; base-uri &#39;none&#39;;</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>form-action &#39;self&#39;; frame-ancestors &#39;none&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Each directive is explained on the <a href="./tanstack-start.html#the-content-security-policy">TanStack Start guide</a>. <code>data:</code> in <code>img-src</code> is for the providers&#39; button logos and is needed only when a provider has an icon; an app whose providers all show letter tiles may leave it out. Send it with <code>Cache-Control: private, no-store</code> and <code>Vary: Cookie</code> on every page response.</p><h2 id="the-twelve-limits" tabindex="-1">The twelve limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-twelve-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The twelve limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>A hostile sibling subdomain can set cookies, but cannot fix a session.</strong> A compromised or hostile subdomain of your site can set cookies your app receives. The cookie reader stops a planted value from being accepted as real: a cookie name that arrives more than once counts as absent, a pair with an empty name is ignored, names are compared byte for byte, and a refresh value is used only when its session id matches the one in <code>uat</code>.</li><li><strong>Reverification is only as strong as the user&#39;s GitHub session.</strong> GitHub returns at once for a user who is signed in and has already authorised the app. A script on the app origin can still navigate to reverification, where the account picker needs a real click on github.com. So reverification limits script injection but does not defeat it. A provider made with <code>oauth2()</code> sends no prompt at all, because plain OAuth 2 has none: a service that remembers the user sends them straight back, so reverification through it proves only that the user still controls their session at that service, not that they signed in again. A provider made with <code>oidc()</code> sends <code>prompt=login</code> and <code>max_age=0</code>, and refuses an id_token whose <code>auth_time</code> is older than the reverification&#39;s start minus <code>clockSkewSeconds</code>; a service that leaves <code>auth_time</code> out gives the same weaker proof as <code>oauth2()</code>. A provider made with <code>google()</code> sends <code>prompt=select_account</code>, because Google offers no <code>prompt=login</code>: a Google reverification confirms which Google account the user holds and does not force a fresh login at Google, so passkey reverification is the strong one. With <code>hostedDomain</code>, <code>google()</code> admits only an id_token whose signed <code>hd</code> claim is exactly that domain, and never judges by the email&#39;s domain.</li><li><strong>Wildcard redirect matching on the GitHub OAuth app must be off.</strong> See the <a href="./github-oauth-app.html">GitHub OAuth app guide</a>.</li><li><strong>All code deployed to your Convex deployment can read the signing key and the master secret.</strong> Treat every dependency and every function there as trusted.</li><li><strong>A client that retries after the grace window has passed is treated as a thief.</strong> Its session is revoked. With the window at 0, any lost response does this.</li><li><strong>The confirm click before account deletion guards against a stray link, not against injected script.</strong> Any script holding the access token can send the delete.</li><li><strong>Users who share one client address can use up each other&#39;s buckets.</strong> The per-address buckets are <code>callback_refusal</code>, <code>self_read</code> (the bucket of <code>GET {basePath}/me</code>, <code>GET {basePath}/sessions</code> and <code>GET {basePath}/passkeys</code>), with passkeys on <code>passkey_signin</code>, which counts every passkey sign-in completion, and, in hub mode, <code>authorize</code>, which counts refusals only. In hub mode the <code>token</code> bucket also counts refusals only, per client and address, so on one address it is shared only among requests that name the same app.</li><li><strong>Anyone holding the master secret can forge a refresh token for a known session id and force that session&#39;s revocation.</strong> A session id is not secret. The nonce is stored hashed, so the master secret together with a copy of the database still yields no usable token.</li><li><strong>Changing <code>basePath</code> to a path nested under the old one signs each user out once.</strong> The browser then sends both refresh values, and a valid old one is reuse.</li><li><strong>A database restore from a backup signs users out.</strong> A user holding a grace sibling issued after the backup is revoked as <code>refresh_reuse</code> instead, because that token carries the restored row&#39;s current generation with a nonce the row does not hold.</li><li><strong>A request carrying only junk refresh values still costs one HTTP action call.</strong> That is host-level abuse the product does not try to solve.</li><li><strong>Someone holding a real token, even one from a revoked session, can make the server do a small fixed set of reads per request.</strong> The reads are the session and its user, and the product puts no limit on them. Raw request volume is left to your host&#39;s rate limiting.</li></ol><h2 id="the-shared-sign-in-server-hub-mode" tabindex="-1">The shared sign-in server (hub mode) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-shared-sign-in-server-hub-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The shared sign-in server (hub mode)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With <code>hub.enabled</code> on, a deployment is also a small OpenID provider for your other Evo Auth apps. Its properties:</p><ul><li><strong>A code is used once, lives 60 seconds and is stored only as a hash.</strong> It is 32 random bytes; only its SHA-256 is stored, and the code itself leaves the hub only in the redirect to the app&#39;s checked <code>redirect_uri</code>, never in a log line or an event. It is bound to the client id, the exact <code>redirect_uri</code>, the PKCE challenge and the nonce. The first attempt to redeem it uses it up, even a refused one, so of two requests for one code at once only one succeeds.</li><li><strong>The token route compares three things, every time:</strong> the client id, the <code>redirect_uri</code> byte for byte as the app wrote it in the authorize request (before any URL parsing, so a default port, a change of letter case or a trailing dot is a different value), and the SHA-256 of the PKCE verifier against the stored challenge, in constant time. Any failure is the same <code>400 invalid_grant</code>, as is a code that is unknown, used or expired, and none says which check failed. A malformed request (a missing or repeated parameter, a <code>code_verifier</code> of the wrong form, a body over 4,096 bytes) or a client the list refuses is <code>400 invalid_request</code>, decided before the code is looked at.</li><li><strong>Only the code flow, with the query response mode, and only PKCE with S256.</strong> No implicit flow, no plain PKCE. <code>nonce</code> and <code>state</code> are required, and the nonce is copied into the id_token; <code>auth_time</code> is always present.</li><li><strong>Only the sites you list, and only their exact callback addresses.</strong> <code>client_id</code> must be a bare origin, https except on <code>localhost</code> and <code>127.0.0.1</code>, matched by an entry of <code>hub.allowedClients</code>; never the hub&#39;s own origin and never a host ending in a dot. <code>redirect_uri</code> must equal that entry&#39;s callback address byte for byte, with a wildcard&#39;s host or a loopback entry&#39;s port taken from <code>client_id</code>: another path on the same site, an added query or a trailing slash is refused, so a page on an allowed site that leaks its address cannot be sent a code. A request that fails either check is refused on the hub&#39;s own page and nothing is sent to the site. Nothing is read from storage for a request before its client passes this check, except the one count its refusal adds to the bucket.</li><li><strong>id_tokens and the hub&#39;s own access tokens never share an audience.</strong> Both are signed with the hub&#39;s signing key. An access token&#39;s <code>aud</code> is <code>appId</code>, which in hub mode can never be an origin; an id_token&#39;s <code>aud</code> is the app&#39;s origin, which can never be the hub&#39;s own. The access-token verifier also requires <code>sid</code>, which an id_token never carries.</li><li><strong>The hub carries no organisation.</strong> Organisations belong to each app. The id_token the hub gives an app never has an <code>o</code> claim, even when the hub&#39;s own session has an active organisation.</li><li><strong>The token route is for the app&#39;s server.</strong> It sends no CORS headers, reads no cookie, and refuses a request whose <code>Origin</code> is not the app&#39;s own origin; a request with no <code>Origin</code>, as a server sends, passes.</li><li><strong>The hub&#39;s pages cannot be framed.</strong> Every answer of the three routes carries <code>Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors &#39;none&#39;</code> and <code>Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code>.</li><li><strong><code>prompt=login</code>, <code>max_age=0</code>, and a <code>max_age</code> the hub session&#39;s last sign-in is older than rerun the sign-in at the hub&#39;s provider.</strong> <code>max_age</code> is compared in whole seconds with the session&#39;s sign-in time, the same value the id_token carries as <code>auth_time</code>, on the hub&#39;s own clock and with no allowance for skew, so the hub&#39;s decision and the app&#39;s own check of <code>auth_time</code> agree. The sign-in is rerun (with more than one, the hub sends the rerun to the provider of the person&#39;s primary account, the one they linked first, and never offers another; a fresh sign-in at any provider linked to the same user satisfies it, as it would on a first sign-in, and a rerun completed through another linked provider is only as fresh as that provider can make it: an <code>oauth2()</code> provider cannot force a new login, as for linking above; nothing becomes weaker, since the person still needs that user&#39;s hub session in that browser and could reach the same provider on a first sign-in), once, so an app&#39;s reverification still means a fresh round trip there; the id_token&#39;s <code>auth_time</code> is that sign-in&#39;s time, and the app&#39;s own check decides whether it is fresh enough. The request for the rerun and its start time are sealed in the hub&#39;s sign-in cookie, bound to that one authorize request, and never put in a URL. Coming back, the hub issues a code only when its session&#39;s sign-in time is at or after the rerun&#39;s start; when the rerun did not move it (someone else signed in at the provider, an account not linked to the hub&#39;s user), the app gets <code>error=login_required</code> with its <code>state</code> and no code is made. Apps should still check <code>auth_time</code>: a browser that drops the cookie, or a request sent without <code>prompt</code> or <code>max_age</code>, gets an ordinary answer.</li><li><strong><code>prompt=none</code> never shows a page.</strong> With a live hub session the app gets a code as without it; with none, or one that would need a sign-in, <code>max_age=0</code> and a <code>max_age</code> the session is too old for included, it gets <code>error=login_required</code> with its <code>state</code>. <code>prompt=none</code> together with <code>login</code> is <code>invalid_request</code>. Other <code>prompt</code> values are ignored.</li><li><strong>Request objects are refused unread.</strong> A request carrying <code>request</code> goes back to the app with <code>error=request_not_supported</code>, and one carrying <code>request_uri</code> with <code>error=request_uri_not_supported</code>, each with its <code>state</code>. Neither value is read, parsed or fetched, so a <code>request_uri</code> cannot make the hub send a request anywhere.</li><li><strong>A banned hub user cannot sign in to any app through the hub.</strong> Their hub session no longer counts, and the hub&#39;s sign-in refuses them. A user banned or deleted between the code and its redemption is refused at redemption.</li><li><strong>Signing out on the hub ends only the hub&#39;s session.</strong> Apps keep their own users and sessions; nothing tells an app, and an app&#39;s session of a banned or deleted hub user stays until it ends there.</li><li><strong>Refusals are limited per client address, and at the token route per app as well.</strong> Each refused request counts toward the <code>authorize</code> or <code>token</code> bucket, 20 in 600 seconds by default, and a success never counts. <code>authorize</code> is kept per address: someone behind one address can delay sign-ins through the hub for the other people on that address, but cannot use up a code, a session or a limit of anyone on another address. <code>token</code> is kept per app (the checked <code>client_id</code>) and address. The token route&#39;s address is the app server&#39;s, and on shared hosting a stranger&#39;s code may send from the same outgoing address as your app&#39;s server: by naming your app in 20 refused requests they can still lock your app&#39;s code exchanges out from that address for up to 600 seconds, but they cannot lock out any other app on that address, cannot redeem your app&#39;s code without its PKCE verifier, and requests whose <code>client_id</code> is not on the list count only toward that address&#39;s own bucket, which no listed app&#39;s exchange uses. The <code>code_verifier</code> must be 43 to 128 unreserved characters, as RFC 7636 writes it; any other is refused before the code is looked at, so it does not use the code up.</li><li><strong>A wildcard is for a domain you own.</strong> <code>https://*.&lt;parent&gt;</code> covers every site one label under the hub&#39;s parent domain. On a shared hosting name (<code>my-hub.vercel.app</code> with <code>https://*.vercel.app</code>) that is every app anyone hosts there; <code>evo-auth check</code> warns for each wildcard.</li></ul><h2 id="the-standalone-server" tabindex="-1">The standalone server <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-standalone-server" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The standalone server&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>@evo-auth/server</code> runs core&#39;s handler on Node over one SQLite file, so every property above holds as it does on Convex. What it adds is the address rules, signed webhooks and a file on disk. The guide is <a href="./server.html">The standalone server</a>.</p><ul><li><strong>It listens on loopback unless told otherwise, and has no TLS.</strong> The port must be given. A host that is not loopback is refused unless <code>listen.public</code> is <code>true</code>. A proxy in front (Caddy, nginx) ends TLS.</li><li><strong>The client address is the connection&#39;s, or the rightmost <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> entry from a listed proxy.</strong> <code>trustedProxies</code> is empty unless set and holds exact IP addresses. The entries and the peer are compared as normalised IP literals, so <code>::ffff:127.0.0.1</code> and <code>127.0.0.1</code> are one address. An entry further left in the header was written by whoever sent the request to the proxy, so it is never read, and a listed proxy that sent no header leaves the request without an address, which core refuses. With <code>EVO_AUTH_PROXY_SECRET</code> set the address comes only from <code>createAuthProxy</code>&#39;s headers and the connection is not read. Outside <code>cookies.devMode</code> the server refuses to start unless one of the two is set.</li><li><strong>Each request&#39;s URL is built from <code>publicUrl</code>&#39;s origin.</strong> A <code>Host</code> header or an absolute request target a client sends does not move a request to another origin: a target that is not a path gets a 400 before core sees it.</li><li><strong>A webhook is signed, so a receiver can tell it is yours.</strong> The signature is HMAC-SHA256 over <code>{id}.{timestamp}.{body}</code> under a secret of 32 random bytes that belongs to one endpoint. During a rotation a delivery carries a signature for each of the two secrets. The URL must be <code>https</code>, or <code>http</code> on loopback, and no redirect is followed.</li><li><strong>What a receiver must check.</strong> Run <code>verifyWebhook</code> on the raw body bytes, before anything parses them: it checks the signature before it reads the body, refuses a timestamp more than 5 minutes from now, compares in constant time and returns the event only after all of that passes. It cannot tell a replay inside the 5 minutes, or a repeated delivery, from the first one, so drop repeats by <code>webhook-id</code>. It does not order events: keep the row with the highest <code>user.updatedAt</code>.</li><li><strong>A webhook body carries the user&#39;s <code>privateMetadata</code> and email.</strong> It is the user as the server API returns them. Deliver only to a receiver you trust with that, over <code>https</code>.</li><li><strong>The file is private to its user.</strong> On a service, the directory is mode <code>0700</code>, the file <code>0600</code> and both are owned by the user <code>evo-auth</code>. The server creates the file, and every copy of it (a backup, the copy before a migration), with mode <code>0600</code> on POSIX. The write-ahead log beside the file is protected by the directory. On Windows the mode restricts nothing and the folder&#39;s permissions decide.</li><li><strong>The file holds the same data as the Convex tables, and a backup is a copy of all of it.</strong> That includes the stored webhook bodies. Keep backups as private as the file.</li><li><strong>Every commit is on disk before it counts</strong> (<code>synchronous=FULL</code>, fixed), so a revoked session, a ban or a revoke-all cannot be lost with the last commit.</li><li><strong>Nothing sensitive is logged.</strong> A log line holds an event name and a reason code only: never an address, a user, an endpoint, a URL, a delivery or a body. A database failure is reported by its SQLite error code alone. A command prints variable names and file names and never a value.</li><li><strong>The secrets are environment variables.</strong> The options file holds none. On a service, keep them in a file only root can read, not in the unit file, which any local user can read with <code>systemctl cat</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="two-known-limits-of-the-cookie-design" tabindex="-1">Two known limits of the cookie design <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-known-limits-of-the-cookie-design" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two known limits of the cookie design&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A second <code>uat</code>.</strong> When a non-conforming browser lets a sibling host plant a second <code>uat</code> beside the real one, the product counts <code>uat</code> as absent and denies the session. It fails closed. This needs both a hostile sibling host and a browser that does not follow the cookie rules.</li><li><strong>No real <code>uat</code> at all.</strong> In a non-conforming browser that lets a sibling host forge host-only cookies, a browser with no real <code>uat</code> can be signed in to the planter&#39;s session. No cookie-only design prevents this, because the attacker can copy every value from their own session. A planted lone session cookie is the attacker&#39;s own and grants nothing beyond that.</li></ul><p>The cookie rules were tested on Chromium and Firefox, where no planted value is read as a real cookie. For Safari the evidence is a published measurement (Squarcina et al., &quot;Cookie Crumbles&quot;, USENIX Security 2023, section 4.2.1), not a run of this product. On Playwright&#39;s Windows WebKit build, which is not a shipping browser, signed out, a planted session token is read as the session cookie and its signature check rejects it.</p><h2 id="where-secrets-live" tabindex="-1">Where secrets live <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-secrets-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where secrets live&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>EVO_AUTH_PASSWORD_PEPPER</code> is its own secret, not derived from the master secret, and it belongs with the auth endpoints only. Replacing it makes every user reset their password: see <a href="./rotating-secrets.html#replacing-evo_auth_password_pepper">Rotating secrets</a>.</li><li><code>EVO_AUTH_SECOND_FACTOR_KEY</code> is its own secret too, not derived from the master secret, and it belongs with the auth endpoints only. It seals every authenticator secret and every backup code. Replacing it is done with a previous key and a reseal, so nobody sets up again: see <a href="./rotating-secrets.html#replacing-evo_auth_second_factor_key">the steps</a>. Without the database, the key opens nothing; without the key, the database opens nothing.</li><li><code>.env.local</code> holds the master secret, the password pepper, the second-factor key, the signing key and the staged key together. Keeping those roles apart therefore depends on the deployment&#39;s own environment, not on this file.</li><li>A key write goes through a temporary file in the same folder, then a rename. An interrupted write (Ctrl+C, or a failed delete on Windows) can leave that temporary file holding a key. If your ignore rules do not cover it, it is unignored. Delete it.</li><li>If a file named by Node&#39;s own <code>--env-file</code> exists, Node loads it before <code>evo-auth</code> refuses that option. Use <code>--file</code> for the key file.</li><li>No command prints a secret or key value, and no log or error message carries one.</li><li>A Convex admin key or deploy key must never reach a browser or a client bundle: <a href="./convex.html#never-let-a-convex-admin-or-deploy-key-reach-the-browser">Convex guide</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="see-also" tabindex="-1">See also <a class="header-anchor" href="#see-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See also&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./rotating-secrets.html">Rotating secrets</a></li><li><a href="./configuration.html">Configuration</a></li><li><a href="./troubleshooting.html">Troubleshooting</a></li></ul>',71)])])}const p=o(a,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
